virtio-blk: do not call every virtual disk a spinning one

A virtio disk that reports no optimal I/O size is marked rotational, and the
block layer then sets read-ahead to twice the largest request: 8 MiB here.
Page faults on a mapped file read around by the same window, so vminitd and
vmexec -- 83 MiB static binaries that need 20-25 MiB each -- arrived in the
guest's page cache at 60 MiB each. 120 MiB of every idle pod VM's 226 MiB was
that cache (experiments/32-pod-memory-footprint), and the host keeps every
guest page it has backed, reclaimable or not.

Without the flag read-ahead is the kernel's 128 KiB default. The disks are
files on the Mac's SSD, so the flag's other meanings (seek-avoiding I/O
scheduling, entropy from disk timing) never applied either. What it costs is
a cold sequential read of a large file, measured at about 3.0 GB/s against
4.9; a privileged pod that wants the rest can raise read_ahead_kb itself.

--- a/drivers/block/virtio_blk.c
+++ b/drivers/block/virtio_blk.c
@@ -1431,7 +1431,6 @@
 {
 	struct virtio_blk *vblk;
 	struct queue_limits lim = {
-		.features		= BLK_FEAT_ROTATIONAL,
 		.logical_block_size	= SECTOR_SIZE,
 	};
 	int err, index;
